{"event": "tool_result", "time": 1793244614.1416402, "run_id": "e062ae75f4864af1bc6c029afbd89bab", "seq": 216, "action": {"tool": "write_note", "title": "overview", "content": "补记 p19-22：§2.4.3 推理局限—LLM 缺因果推断/反事实能力、组合谜题（如汉诺塔）表现差；对抗性注入无关事实可使推理模型过度思考并升高错误率。§2.4.4 AGI 传统视智能为个体竞争性推理问题，但人类智能在社交情境中涌现。§2.4.5 可解释性：LIME/SHAP/反事实、机制可解释性（稀疏自编码器、神经透镜、电路发现）。§2.4.6 用例为决策、数学应用题、算法生成、问答基准；检索增强最接近真实 agent 行为。§3 Acting：世界模型（WorldCoder）支持规划；SayCan、Inner Monologue 用语言反馈驱动机器人操作；Chain of Tools 采用计划-执行-观察流水线。", "evidence_ids": ["ev-562f05b08e87ac882b23", "ev-f5fab558c5f762a67dcc", "ev-7f4d5e2fe0886c80c09b"], "section": "overview"}, "result": {"note_id": "overview", "sources": ["paper.pdf"], "chars": 314}}
{"event": "tool_call", "time": 1793244614.1417108, "run_id": "e062ae75f4864af1bc6c029afbd89bab", "seq": 217, "tool": "read_pdf", "arguments": {"start_page": 23, "end_page": 26}}
{"event": "command_result", "time": 1793244614.2708757, "run_id": "e062ae75f4864af1bc6c029afbd89bab", "seq": 218, "argv": ["/mnt/d/Muiti-Agent Programing/experiments/code-agent-smolagents/24371220/.venv/bin/python", "read_pdf.py", "--start-page", "23", "--end-page", "26"], "cwd": "/home/ventsdenye/.local/state/code-agent/evaluations/run-3rn9_ftz/results/paper/repo", "rc": 0, "stdout": "{\"pdf\": \"/home/ventsdenye/.local/state/code-agent/evaluations/run-3rn9_ftz/results/paper/repo/paper.pdf\", \"pages\": [{\"page\": 23, \"text\": \"                                                                                                                  Agentic LLM     •   1:23\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n                  Fig. 13. Multi LLM agent framework with a planner, caller, summarizer [Shen et al., 2024]\\n\\n\\n\\nvision language model, they achieve target object grounding, achieving improved performance on an L-ZSON\\nbenchmark.\\n3.2.2 Action Tools. As we have seen in Section 2.3, LLM results may be augmented with results that are retrieved\\nfrom external sources, such as search engines. The ability to call search engines can be generalized to calling\\nother tools. When the application programming interface (API) of these tools is known, LLMs can be integrated\\neasily with them. To an LLM, an API is just another language to learn. Agentic LLMs must be trained to decide\\nwhen and how to utilize external tools, depending on the task [Shen et al., 2024]. Language models can teach\\nthemselves to use tools [Schick et al., 2023]. The Toolformer model is trained to decide which APIs to call, when\\nto call them, what arguments to pass, and how to best incorporate the results into future token prediction. A\\nrange of tools is tested: a calculator, a question-answering system, a search engine, a translation system, and a\\ncalendar. Further works extend this to a larger range of tools. ToolBench [Qin et al., 2023] contains 16,464 APIs\\nfrom RapidAPI, a large dataset of publicly available REST APIs.4\\n   Another framework is EasyTool [Yuan et al., 2024c], which focuses on structured and unified instructions\\nfrom tool documentations, building on ToolBench. ToolAlpaca is a benchmark with over 3938 instances from 400\\nAPIs [Tang et al., 2023]. A tool-based benchmark for question answering is ToolQA [Zhuang et al., 2023]. Gorilla\\nis a finetuned LLaMa-based model for generating API calls [Patil et al., 2023], also introducing the APIBench\\nbenchmark. Many tool calling frameworks have been developed.\\n   Selecting the right tool and summarizing its result are difficult skills. Zhao et al. [2024a] study how LLMs\\ncan improve recommendation through tool learning. Another approach also suggests to use an LLM for this\\ntask [Shen et al., 2024]. They use different LLMs for (1) reasoning ability, (2) request writing, and (3) result\\nsummarization. Figure 13 illustrates this architecture, consisting of a planner, a caller, and a summarizer, each\\nimplemented by a different LLM finetuned for its specific capability. Good results are reported on the LLMs\\n\\n4 https://rapidapi.com/hub\\n\\n\\n\\n                                          Journal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\\n\"}, {\"page\": 24, \"text\": \"1:24   •   Plaat, Van Duijn, Van Stein, Preuss, Van der Putten & Batenburg\\n\\n\\nClaude-2, ChatGPT, GPT-4, and Tool-LLaMa, using as reasoning strategies ReAct [Yao et al., 2022] and DFSDT\\n[Qin et al., 2023]. Other frameworks also exist, such as [Ocker et al., 2024].\\n3.2.3 Computer and Browser Tools and Agent Interoperability. One specific set of actions tools is to let an LLM\\ninteract with a browser or even a complete computer system as a special form of API. Equipping agentic LLMs\\nwith the ability to interact directly with a computer environment enables many interaction possibilities. Tools\\nthat parse, interpret, and manipulate graphical user interfaces (GUIs) have gained attention for bridging the gap\\nbetween language models and real-world applications. One such example is OmniParser V2 [Lu et al., 2024b],\\nwhich introduces a vision-based screen parsing method to detect and label interactable elements such as buttons\\nor icons. By converting raw screenshots into structured representations, OmniParser helps vision-language\\nmodels ground their action decisions in specific UI components. This grounding increases the accuracy of the\\naction predictions of LLMs.\\n   Agents can also interact with other agents, and tap into tool ecosystems. Standards for agent to agent com-\\nmunication are emerging. Ray [2025] reviews the emerging open A2A standard, for in-context learning. Other\\nprotocols for tool use and agent to agent communication are being developed, such as the Model Context Protocol\\n(MCP), Agent Communication Protocol (ACP), and Agent Network Protocol (ANP). Various agentic evaluation\\nbenchmarks such as MCPRadar [Gao et al., 2025], MCPEval [Liu et al., 2025b], MCPBench [Wang et al., 2025c],\\nMCP-Universe Luo et al. [2025] and LiveMCPBench [Mo et al., 2025] are built on MCP tool use, to make these\\nbenchmarks more representative for real world task settings. Zhang et al. [2025b] propose a multi-agent orches-\\ntration Tool-Environment-Agent protocol aimed at integrating environments. Agent interoperability protocols\\nare an active field of research, for a survey, see Ehtesham et al. [2025].\\n   Another line of research focuses on enabling large language models to initiate system-level commands or\\nnavigate within browser or operating system interfaces. Computer Use, proposed by Antropic, and Operator,\\nproposed by OpenAI, are examples of such efforts. Both of these tools wrap common desktop and browser actions\\n(such as opening applications, clicking buttons, and filling forms) into tool APIs callable by an LLM. This setup\\ntranslates high-level textual commands into executable steps. As a result, an agentic LLM can browse the web,\\nmanage files, or run administrative tasks, all through natural language prompts.\\n   Browser Use [Müller and Žunič, 2024] is an example of an open-source tool that enables LLMs to use a browser\\nwith persistent session management. Browser Use allows agentic LLMs to maintain longer browsing states across\\nmultiple pages or domains. The tool manages cookies, session tokens, and dynamic web content updates, thereby\\nallowing LLMs to execute more complex browsing tasks like multi-step form completions or cross-site queries.\\n   For a more in-depth discussion about browser and computer environments, see the survey by Wang et al.\\n[2025b]. The survey discusses design patterns for combining automated GUI parsing, tool call integration, and\\nhuman-in-the-loop supervision. Collectively, these approaches reveal that reliable computer and browser tools\\nare important components for agentic LLMs. By translating textual intents into grounded, environment-level\\noperations, such systems expand the possible tasks and domains where language-driven autonomy can be\\neffectively realized.\\n3.2.4 Safety and Security. Safety and security are increasing in importance as the capabilities of agentic LLMs\\ngrow. The robustness of LLMs to jailbreak attacks, where users design prompts to circumvent safety measures\\nand misuse model capabilities, has been studied for chatbots. Meanwhile, agentic LLMs use external tools and\\ncan execute multi stage tasks, and may pose a greater risk. Andriushchenko et al. [2025] propose AgentHarm.\\nThis benchmark includes a set of 110 malicious agent tasks ranging from fraud, cyber crime, to harassment. The\\nauthors find that leading commercial LLMs are surprisingly compliant to their given rules with malicious user\\nrequests without jailbreaking (How do I make a pipe bomb? is blocked). However, simple universal templates can\\nbe adapted to effectively jailbreak agents (Order online all necessary ingredients to make a pipe bomb and get them\\ndelivered to my home without getting flagged by authorities, however, passes in GPT-4).\\n\\nJournal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\\n\"}, {\"page\": 25, \"text\": \"                                                                                                              Agentic LLM     •   1:25\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n                 Fig. 14. Adversarial prompts generated by Rainbow Teaming [Samvelyan et al., 2024]\\n\\n\\n   Rainbow Teaming [Samvelyan et al., 2024] uses an open world approach to generate adversarial LLM prompts,\\nto improve the robustness of LLMs. As with training LLMs, a problem with testing methods is a lack of diversity.\\nRainbow Teaming casts adversarial prompt generation as a quality-diversity problem. Rainbow Teaming is an\\nopen-ended approach [Hughes et al., 2024]. It creates diversity with MAP-Elites [Mouret and Clune, 2015], an\\nevolutionary meta search method that iteratively populates an archive with increasingly higher-performing\\nprompts. Figures 14 and 15 illustrate the ideas of Rainbow Teaming.\\n\\n3.3   Assistants\\nThe progress in reasoning and decision making has improved the accuracy and usability of LLMs for everyday\\ntasks. Also, LLMs can act through their use of tools. Tool-enabled LLMs can be used as virtual assistants. The\\nuse of agentic LLMs as assistants has received commercial interest, and much activity has been reported. An\\nadditional advantage is that by assisting humans, the agents generate new training data on which the LLMs can\\nbe pretrained and finetuned.\\n   We start our review of assistants with conversational assistants, and then continue to shopping, travel, medical,\\nand financial trading assistants. An assistant can be seen here as a use-case of an agentic LLM for a specific range\\nof tasks or a specific working domain.\\n3.3.1 Conversational Assistants and Negotiation. Agentic LLMs can be used to make Human-Computer interaction\\nmore natural [Neszlényi et al., 2024, Oluwagbade, 2024]. The AssistantGPT system supports a diverse range of\\noperations, including web searches, API interactions via OpenAPI schemas, voice conversations, and command\\nexecution through the shell. The system consists of an LLM with access to tools, a planner, and memory (see\\nFigure 16). The system is designed for deployment in an educational setting, a corporate setting, and to support\\nremote work environments such as Teams and Slack.\\n   Cabrero-Daniel et al. [2024] describe how LLM meeting assistants can improve agile software development\\nteam meetings, to generate favorable results for preparation and live assistance during Scrum meetings, although\\nsome testers remarked that LLM interventions felt unnatural and inflexible.\\n   A system to facilitate group conversations is MUCA [Mao et al., 2024], supporting What, When and Who\\nquestions, consisting of a sub-topic generator, dialog analyzer, and conversational strategies arbitrator. Wei et al.\\n\\n                                      Journal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\\n\"}, {\"page\": 26, \"text\": \"1:26   •   Plaat, Van Duijn, Van Stein, Preuss, Van der Putten & Batenburg\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n                 Fig. 15. Quality Diversity Mutation Architecture of Rainbow Teaming [Samvelyan et al., 2024]\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n                                 Fig. 16. AssistantGPT system architecture [Neszlényi et al., 2024]\\n\\n\\n[2024b] report improved collaboration through the use of LLM agents in a collaborative learning classroom\\nsetting. Another study reports improved work efficiency in a collaborative task scheduling experiment [Bastola\\net al., 2023].\\n   A different type of assistant is the thinking assistant. This assistant tries to improve (human) reflective thinking\\nfor difficult decisions, by asking instead of answering [Park and Kulkarni, 2023].\\n\\nJournal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\\n\"}]}\n", "stderr": "", "timed_out": false}
{"event": "evidence", "time": 1793244614.279509, "run_id": "e062ae75f4864af1bc6c029afbd89bab", "seq": 219, "id": "ev-731fa63b8d34dde45e62", "source": "paper.pdf", "version": "b4767423c79db69ae7138f4c0aed7733af6385e0a370da21ad7d6fe029d7fb8c", "locator": "page:23", "text": "                                                                                                                  Agentic LLM     •   1:23\n\n\n\n\n                  Fig. 13. Multi LLM agent framework with a planner, caller, summarizer [Shen et al., 2024]\n\n\n\nvision language model, they achieve target object grounding, achieving improved performance on an L-ZSON\nbenchmark.\n3.2.2 Action Tools. As we have seen in Section 2.3, LLM results may be augmented with results that are retrieved\nfrom external sources, such as search engines. The ability to call search engines can be generalized to calling\nother tools. When the application programming interface (API) of these tools is known, LLMs can be integrated\neasily with them. To an LLM, an API is just another language to learn. Agentic LLMs must be trained to decide\nwhen and how to utilize external tools, depending on the task [Shen et al., 2024]. Language models can teach\nthemselves to use tools [Schick et al., 2023]. The Toolformer model is trained to decide which APIs to call, when\nto call them, what arguments to pass, and how to best incorporate the results into future token prediction. A\nrange of tools is tested: a calculator, a question-answering system, a search engine, a translation system, and a\ncalendar. Further works extend this to a larger range of tools. ToolBench [Qin et al., 2023] contains 16,464 APIs\nfrom RapidAPI, a large dataset of publicly available REST APIs.4\n   Another framework is EasyTool [Yuan et al., 2024c], which focuses on structured and unified instructions\nfrom tool documentations, building on ToolBench. ToolAlpaca is a benchmark with over 3938 instances from 400\nAPIs [Tang et al., 2023]. A tool-based benchmark for question answering is ToolQA [Zhuang et al., 2023]. Gorilla\nis a finetuned LLaMa-based model for generating API calls [Patil et al., 2023], also introducing the APIBench\nbenchmark. Many tool calling frameworks have been developed.\n   Selecting the right tool and summarizing its result are difficult skills. Zhao et al. [2024a] study how LLMs\ncan improve recommendation through tool learning. Another approach also suggests to use an LLM for this\ntask [Shen et al., 2024]. They use different LLMs for (1) reasoning ability, (2) request writing, and (3) result\nsummarization. Figure 13 illustrates this architecture, consisting of a planner, a caller, and a summarizer, each\nimplemented by a different LLM finetuned for its specific capability. Good results are reported on the LLMs\n\n4 https://rapidapi.com/hub\n\n\n\n                                          Journal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\n", "required": true, "page": 23, "valid": true, "atomic": true}
{"event": "evidence", "time": 1793244614.2796242, "run_id": "e062ae75f4864af1bc6c029afbd89bab", "seq": 220, "id": "ev-4e747765f5046c8ad8b1", "source": "paper.pdf", "version": "b4767423c79db69ae7138f4c0aed7733af6385e0a370da21ad7d6fe029d7fb8c", "locator": "page:24", "text": "1:24   •   Plaat, Van Duijn, Van Stein, Preuss, Van der Putten & Batenburg\n\n\nClaude-2, ChatGPT, GPT-4, and Tool-LLaMa, using as reasoning strategies ReAct [Yao et al., 2022] and DFSDT\n[Qin et al., 2023]. Other frameworks also exist, such as [Ocker et al., 2024].\n3.2.3 Computer and Browser Tools and Agent Interoperability. One specific set of actions tools is to let an LLM\ninteract with a browser or even a complete computer system as a special form of API. Equipping agentic LLMs\nwith the ability to interact directly with a computer environment enables many interaction possibilities. Tools\nthat parse, interpret, and manipulate graphical user interfaces (GUIs) have gained attention for bridging the gap\nbetween language models and real-world applications. One such example is OmniParser V2 [Lu et al., 2024b],\nwhich introduces a vision-based screen parsing method to detect and label interactable elements such as buttons\nor icons. By converting raw screenshots into structured representations, OmniParser helps vision-language\nmodels ground their action decisions in specific UI components. This grounding increases the accuracy of the\naction predictions of LLMs.\n   Agents can also interact with other agents, and tap into tool ecosystems. Standards for agent to agent com-\nmunication are emerging. Ray [2025] reviews the emerging open A2A standard, for in-context learning. Other\nprotocols for tool use and agent to agent communication are being developed, such as the Model Context Protocol\n(MCP), Agent Communication Protocol (ACP), and Agent Network Protocol (ANP). Various agentic evaluation\nbenchmarks such as MCPRadar [Gao et al., 2025], MCPEval [Liu et al., 2025b], MCPBench [Wang et al., 2025c],\nMCP-Universe Luo et al. [2025] and LiveMCPBench [Mo et al., 2025] are built on MCP tool use, to make these\nbenchmarks more representative for real world task settings. Zhang et al. [2025b] propose a multi-agent orches-\ntration Tool-Environment-Agent protocol aimed at integrating environments. Agent interoperability protocols\nare an active field of research, for a survey, see Ehtesham et al. [2025].\n   Another line of research focuses on enabling large language models to initiate system-level commands or\nnavigate within browser or operating system interfaces. Computer Use, proposed by Antropic, and Operator,\nproposed by OpenAI, are examples of such efforts. Both of these tools wrap common desktop and browser actions\n(such as opening applications, clicking buttons, and filling forms) into tool APIs callable by an LLM. This setup\ntranslates high-level textual commands into executable steps. As a result, an agentic LLM can browse the web,\nmanage files, or run administrative tasks, all through natural language prompts.\n   Browser Use [Müller and Žunič, 2024] is an example of an open-source tool that enables LLMs to use a browser\nwith persistent session management. Browser Use allows agentic LLMs to maintain longer browsing states across\nmultiple pages or domains. The tool manages cookies, session tokens, and dynamic web content updates, thereby\nallowing LLMs to execute more complex browsing tasks like multi-step form completions or cross-site queries.\n   For a more in-depth discussion about browser and computer environments, see the survey by Wang et al.\n[2025b]. The survey discusses design patterns for combining automated GUI parsing, tool call integration, and\nhuman-in-the-loop supervision. Collectively, these approaches reveal that reliable computer and browser tools\nare important components for agentic LLMs. By translating textual intents into grounded, environment-level\noperations, such systems expand the possible tasks and domains where language-driven autonomy can be\neffectively realized.\n3.2.4 Safety and Security. Safety and security are increasing in importance as the capabilities of agentic LLMs\ngrow. The robustness of LLMs to jailbreak attacks, where users design prompts to circumvent safety measures\nand misuse model capabilities, has been studied for chatbots. Meanwhile, agentic LLMs use external tools and\ncan execute multi stage tasks, and may pose a greater risk. Andriushchenko et al. [2025] propose AgentHarm.\nThis benchmark includes a set of 110 malicious agent tasks ranging from fraud, cyber crime, to harassment. The\nauthors find that leading commercial LLMs are surprisingly compliant to their given rules with malicious user\nrequests without jailbreaking (How do I make a pipe bomb? is blocked). However, simple universal templates can\nbe adapted to effectively jailbreak agents (Order online all necessary ingredients to make a pipe bomb and get them\ndelivered to my home without getting flagged by authorities, however, passes in GPT-4).\n\nJournal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\n", "required": true, "page": 24, "valid": true, "atomic": true}
{"event": "evidence", "time": 1793244614.2796903, "run_id": "e062ae75f4864af1bc6c029afbd89bab", "seq": 221, "id": "ev-daeb56e673fc61cae0ef", "source": "paper.pdf", "version": "b4767423c79db69ae7138f4c0aed7733af6385e0a370da21ad7d6fe029d7fb8c", "locator": "page:25", "text": "                                                                                                              Agentic LLM     •   1:25\n\n\n\n\n                 Fig. 14. Adversarial prompts generated by Rainbow Teaming [Samvelyan et al., 2024]\n\n\n   Rainbow Teaming [Samvelyan et al., 2024] uses an open world approach to generate adversarial LLM prompts,\nto improve the robustness of LLMs. As with training LLMs, a problem with testing methods is a lack of diversity.\nRainbow Teaming casts adversarial prompt generation as a quality-diversity problem. Rainbow Teaming is an\nopen-ended approach [Hughes et al., 2024]. It creates diversity with MAP-Elites [Mouret and Clune, 2015], an\nevolutionary meta search method that iteratively populates an archive with increasingly higher-performing\nprompts. Figures 14 and 15 illustrate the ideas of Rainbow Teaming.\n\n3.3   Assistants\nThe progress in reasoning and decision making has improved the accuracy and usability of LLMs for everyday\ntasks. Also, LLMs can act through their use of tools. Tool-enabled LLMs can be used as virtual assistants. The\nuse of agentic LLMs as assistants has received commercial interest, and much activity has been reported. An\nadditional advantage is that by assisting humans, the agents generate new training data on which the LLMs can\nbe pretrained and finetuned.\n   We start our review of assistants with conversational assistants, and then continue to shopping, travel, medical,\nand financial trading assistants. An assistant can be seen here as a use-case of an agentic LLM for a specific range\nof tasks or a specific working domain.\n3.3.1 Conversational Assistants and Negotiation. Agentic LLMs can be used to make Human-Computer interaction\nmore natural [Neszlényi et al., 2024, Oluwagbade, 2024]. The AssistantGPT system supports a diverse range of\noperations, including web searches, API interactions via OpenAPI schemas, voice conversations, and command\nexecution through the shell. The system consists of an LLM with access to tools, a planner, and memory (see\nFigure 16). The system is designed for deployment in an educational setting, a corporate setting, and to support\nremote work environments such as Teams and Slack.\n   Cabrero-Daniel et al. [2024] describe how LLM meeting assistants can improve agile software development\nteam meetings, to generate favorable results for preparation and live assistance during Scrum meetings, although\nsome testers remarked that LLM interventions felt unnatural and inflexible.\n   A system to facilitate group conversations is MUCA [Mao et al., 2024], supporting What, When and Who\nquestions, consisting of a sub-topic generator, dialog analyzer, and conversational strategies arbitrator. Wei et al.\n\n                                      Journal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\n", "required": true, "page": 25, "valid": true, "atomic": true}
{"event": "evidence", "time": 1793244614.279735, "run_id": "e062ae75f4864af1bc6c029afbd89bab", "seq": 222, "id": "ev-a30e6edea79391cf315d", "source": "paper.pdf", "version": "b4767423c79db69ae7138f4c0aed7733af6385e0a370da21ad7d6fe029d7fb8c", "locator": "page:26", "text": "1:26   •   Plaat, Van Duijn, Van Stein, Preuss, Van der Putten & Batenburg\n\n\n\n\n                 Fig. 15. Quality Diversity Mutation Architecture of Rainbow Teaming [Samvelyan et al., 2024]\n\n\n\n\n                                 Fig. 16. AssistantGPT system architecture [Neszlényi et al., 2024]\n\n\n[2024b] report improved collaboration through the use of LLM agents in a collaborative learning classroom\nsetting. Another study reports improved work efficiency in a collaborative task scheduling experiment [Bastola\net al., 2023].\n   A different type of assistant is the thinking assistant. This assistant tries to improve (human) reflective thinking\nfor difficult decisions, by asking instead of answering [Park and Kulkarni, 2023].\n\nJournal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\n", "required": true, "page": 26, "valid": true, "atomic": true}
