{"event": "tool_result", "time": 1793244747.8603735, "run_id": "e062ae75f4864af1bc6c029afbd89bab", "seq": 233, "action": {"tool": "write_note", "title": "tool_use", "content": "Table 2（p10）Toolformer 让模型自学调用 API：calculator、question answering、search engine、translation、calendar；ToolBench 收录 RapidAPI 的 16,464 个 APIs（p23）；ToolAlpaca 400 APIs、3938 实例，Gorilla 生成 API 调用（APIBench，p23）。EasyTool 基于工具文档统一指令（p23）。Shen 2024 用 planner/caller/summarizer 三 LLM 协作（p23-24）。§3.2.3 计算机/浏览器工具：OmniParser V2 视觉解析 GUI，Computer Use、Operator、Browser Use 包装桌面/浏览动作；A2A、MCP、ACP、ANP 互操作协议（p24）。§3.2.4 安全：AgentHarm 110 个恶意任务，简单模板即可越狱 agent（p24）。p25-26 涉及多智能体评测与 Rainbow Teaming（p26 Fig15）、AssistantGPT 架构（p26 Fig16）、助手提升协作效率与“思考助手”以提问促反思（p26）。", "evidence_ids": ["ev-731fa63b8d34dde45e62", "ev-4e747765f5046c8ad8b1", "ev-a30e6edea79391cf315d"], "section": "tool_use"}, "result": {"note_id": "tool_use", "sources": ["paper.pdf"], "chars": 542}}
{"event": "tool_call", "time": 1793244747.8605182, "run_id": "e062ae75f4864af1bc6c029afbd89bab", "seq": 234, "tool": "read_pdf", "arguments": {"start_page": 27, "end_page": 30}}
{"event": "command_result", "time": 1793244749.1056638, "run_id": "e062ae75f4864af1bc6c029afbd89bab", "seq": 235, "argv": ["/mnt/d/Muiti-Agent Programing/experiments/code-agent-smolagents/24371220/.venv/bin/python", "read_pdf.py", "--start-page", "27", "--end-page", "30"], "cwd": "/home/ventsdenye/.local/state/code-agent/evaluations/run-3rn9_ftz/results/paper/repo", "rc": 0, "stdout": "{\"pdf\": \"/home/ventsdenye/.local/state/code-agent/evaluations/run-3rn9_ftz/results/paper/repo/paper.pdf\", \"pages\": [{\"page\": 27, \"text\": \"                                                                                                              Agentic LLM     •   1:27\\n\\n\\n   Another kind of assistants are research agents, that gather information by combining information from\\ndifferent tools and knowledge sources based on context, and synthesize and summarize the feedback based on\\nthe information need. For instance, Vogt et al. [2025] developed a REWOO-based process mining assistant that\\ncombines a process discovery agent to discover processes and inefficiencies with parallel process research agents,\\nan optional human-in-the-loop to interpret results from a domain-specific real-world knowledge perspective,\\nand a reporting agent to summarize results. Research agents are popular initial use cases. They are relatively low\\nrisk, as actions are limited to various tools to gather data and content, and they build on the strengths of LLMs to\\nanalyze intent and synthesize information rather than being the knowledge source itself.\\n   Conversational assistants have mostly grown out of regular LLMs, sometimes finetuned, grounded and\\ncustomized for a particular area of expertise or domain. Some approaches use a specialized multi-LLMs approach,\\nspecializing LLMs for different sub-tasks.\\n   Shopping Assistants. LLM-based shopping assistants grow out of regular LLMs that are often finetuned on the\\ndomain or task at hand, and may be combined with a recommender system. Retrieval augmentation, tool use,\\nand Chain of Thought are used to improve the performance of shopping assistants.\\n   Basic LLMs generally lack inherent knowledge of e-commerce concepts. Jin et al. [2024b] created the Multi task\\nOnline Shopping Benchmark. Shopping MMLU consists of 57 tasks covering 4 major shopping skills: concept\\nunderstanding, knowledge reasoning, user behavior alignment, and multi-linguality. Vedula et al. [2024] provide\\nquestion suggestion for shopping assistants based on product metadata. ChatShop presents evaluation focused on\\ninformation-seeking [Chen et al., 2024a]. Zhang et al. [2024a] created an E-commerce shopping assistant named\\nLLaSa. They created an instruction dataset comprising 65,000 samples and diverse tasks, and trained the model\\nthrough instruction tuning. The system scores high on the ShopBench benchmark.\\n   Automated negotiation between agents has been studied extensively in AI [Jonker et al., 2017]. LLM based\\nnegotiation introduces a risk of unexpected bias. A study by Kirshner et al. [2025] notes a tendency towards reach-\\ning agreement, which may influence contract terms. A further experimental analysis finds risks of overspending\\nor unreasonable deals for automated negotiation [Zhu et al., 2025]. Section 4.1.2 also discusses negotiation, in a\\nsocial setting.\\n   Flight Operations Assistants. Assistants for booking flights are related to shopping assistants. [Manasa et al.,\\n2024] have developed a flight booking assistant based on LLaMa 2 and RAG. In user testing the system scored\\npositive in understanding user preferences and efficient completion of the booking process.\\n   In specialized domains, operations support assistant have been developed. For example, to automate flight\\nplanning under wind hazards [Tabrizian et al., 2024], and for flight arrival scheduling [Zhou et al., 2024b]. Wassim\\net al. [2024] introduce Drone-as-a-Service operations from text user requests. As agentic LLM technology matures,\\nmore specialized domain assistants will be developed.\\n3.3.2 Medical Assistants. The field of medicine has shown great interest in LLMs [Thirunavukarasu et al., 2023,\\nClusmann et al., 2023, Mehandru et al., 2024]. A recent study showed LLMs scoring higher on diagnoses than\\ntrained human doctors [Goh et al., 2024]. In medical conversations, for medical note generation, LLMs are also\\nexceeding the performance of human scribes [Yuan et al., 2024a]. Another study finds similar results, but also\\npoints to shortcomings in specific areas [Panagoulias et al., 2024].\\n   Sudarshan et al. [2024] report on an experiment with an agentic workflow for generating patient-friendly\\nmedical reports, using the Reflexion approach (Section 2.2.2, [Shinn et al., 2024]), to comply with the 21th Century\\nCures Act that grants patients the right to access their health record data.\\n   A study by Qiu et al. [2024b] reports a wealth of opportunities for LLMs in medicine, ranging from clinical\\nworkflow automation to multi-agent aided diagnosis. Ullah et al. [2024] provide a scoping review on the use of\\nChatGPT for diagnostic medicine. Their main conclusion is that medical and ethical knowledge is necessary\\n\\n                                      Journal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\\n\"}, {\"page\": 28, \"text\": \"1:28   •   Plaat, Van Duijn, Van Stein, Preuss, Van der Putten & Batenburg\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n                                        Fig. 17. Medical Education Copilot [Wei et al., 2024a]\\n\\n\\n\\nwhen training and finetuning these models. A challenge for the adoption of LLM in medicine are concerns about\\nthe quality, accuracy, and the comprehensiveness of LLM-generated answers. Das et al. [2024] describe how to\\nmitigate common pitfalls such as hallucinations, incoherence, and lost-in-the-middle problems. They do so by\\nimplementing an agentic architecture, changing the LLM’s role from directly generating answers, to that of a\\nplanner in a retrieval system. The LLM-agent orchestrates a suite of specialized tools that retrieve information\\nfrom various sources.\\n   In the domain of medical education, Wei et al. [2024a] use a multi agent framework to create copilots that\\nemulate extensive real-world medical training environments (see Figure 17). A benchmark for retrieval-augmented\\ngeneration in the medical domain is [Qiao et al., 2024].\\n\\n3.3.3 Science Assistants. The workflow of scientific experimentation is relatively standardized in certain fields\\nof science. For example, in machine learning, ideas are generated, a hypothesis is formulated, an experiment\\nis designed, datasets are acquired, experiments are performed, results are interpreted and a report is produced.\\nGoogle and OpenAI have both released Deep Research agents. These agents can perform multi-step research\\ntasks, synthesizing online information. They are built with a reasoning LLM and use retrieval augmentation for\\nfinding information sources. The systems are able to create papers that look impressive, but may contain errors,\\nas also indicated by the accompanying disclaimers. This workflow has attracted researchers to experiment with\\nagentic LLMs, see Eger et al. [2025] for a survey.\\n   AI Scientist [Lu et al., 2024a] is a framework to automate the process of scientific discovery, from idea generation\\nto paper writing, including a review process. Users must specify a topic, and provide an experimentation template\\nand indicate datasets. The authors report experiments in three areas of machine learning: diffusion modeling,\\ntransformer-based language modeling, and learning dynamics, with promising results. To improve idea generation\\nand reviewing, the agent accesses open sources. Current limitations include limited experiments, incorrect\\nimplementation of ideas, and visual errors when the paper is produced. Rarely are entire results hallucinated.\\nResults of the AI scientist are recommended to be taken as hints of promising ideas, worthy of a follow up study\\n[Lu et al., 2024a].\\n\\nJournal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\\n\"}, {\"page\": 29, \"text\": \"                                                                                                              Agentic LLM     •   1:29\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n                           Fig. 18. SWE-Agent for software engineering [Yang et al., 2024b]\\n\\n\\n\\n   A similar approach was taken in Agent Laboratory, where the agentic system was positioned as a crew of\\nresearch assistants, working guided by human researchers across literature review, experimentation, and report\\nwriting, and producing experiments, code repositories and a final report [Schmidgall et al., 2025]. The results\\nwere evaluated through a survey. Early human involvement was found to improve the quality of research. The\\nauthors claim that the generated code outperformed prior results, with a substantial reduction in research effort.\\n   SWE-Agent (short for software engineering agent) [Yang et al., 2024b] aims to automate the process of software\\ndiscovery, to help agents to autonomously use computers to solve software engineering tasks. SWE-agent\\nintroduces tools to create and edit code files, navigate through software repositories, and execute computer\\nprograms. Experiments on coding benchmarks such as HumanEvalFix achieve high success rates of over 80%.\\nThis success is attributed to the interactive design of the agent (see Figure 18).\\n   MLGym [Nathani et al., 2025] follows the popular Gym reinforcement learning framework [Brockman et al.,\\n2016]. Gym provides a standardized interface between environment and agent. Introduced in 2016, it accelerated\\nthe development of reinforcement learning algorithms, facilitating progress in the field. Taking further inspiration\\nfrom SWE-agent (such as file editing capabilities) the MLGym work shows how the process of scientific discovery\\ncan be modeled as an interactive process. Applications are discussed in fields ranging from data science, game\\ntheory, computer vision, reinforcement learning, to natural language processing. Experiments are reported with\\ncommercial LLMs (OpenAI, Meta, Google, and Anthropic).\\n   Science assistants are moving from isolated simulations to human/agent research collaborations. Gottweis et al.\\n[2025] describe an attempt to discover significant scientific knowledge and validate these findings in real world\\nexperiments in their AI Co-scientist work. Given a general research goal or idea, AI Co-scientist uses multiple\\nagents for idea generation, reflection, ranking, evolution, proximity and meta-review, ultimately to generate\\nresearch hypotheses and plans. Such collaborations were validated in real world laboratory experiments in drug\\nrepurposing, novel treatment targets and explaining specific mechanism in gene transfer evolution related to\\nmicrobiological resistance [Gottweis et al., 2025, He et al., 2025, Penades et al., 2025].\\n   Another example of real world validation is The Virtual Lab. This approach employs a PI agent directing a crew\\nof specialized agents in chemistry, computer science and bioinformatics tools, that collaborate with a human\\nresearcher to identify new SARS-CoV-2 nanobodies. Promising results in experimental validation were reported\\n[Swanson et al., 2024].\\n\\n3.3.4 Trading Assistants. Another important specialized domain is financial trading. Already many algorithms\\nare used in financial organizations to support trading decisions. The interest in agentic LLMs in the financial\\nworld is large [Ding et al., 2024].\\n   InvestorBench is a benchmark for financial trading systems [Li et al., 2024a]. FinAgent is a tool-augmented\\nmultimodal agent for financial trading [Zhang et al., 2024b]. It contains a market intelligence module, which\\nis able to extract insights from multi-modal datasets of asset prices, visual representations, news, and expert\\n\\n                                      Journal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\\n\"}, {\"page\": 30, \"text\": \"1:30   •    Plaat, Van Duijn, Van Stein, Preuss, Van der Putten & Batenburg\\n\\n\\nanalyses. The system can also perform query retrieval, and performs reflection in a low-level module for technical\\nanalysis, and in a high-level module to analyze past trading decisions.\\n   FinRobot is an agentic LLM for financial analysis, to assist human traders. [Yang et al., 2024a]. FinRobot can\\nprovide document analysis and generation, and market forecasts for individual stocks. FinMem is an agentic\\nLLM framework devised for financial decision-making [Yu et al., 2024b]. It features a layered memory system\\nand adjustable character design for the trading agent. FinMem is inspired by the generative agents framework by\\nPark et al. [2023] (see Section 4.3).\\n   So far, most financial market machine learning has focused on single agent systems. An approach called\\nTradingAgents uses a multi agent system to replicate real-world trading firms’ collaborative dynamics [Xiao\\net al., 2024]. TradingAgents simulates LLM-powered agents in specialized roles such as fundamental analysts,\\nsentiment analysts, technical analysts, and traders with varied risk profiles. The outcome of the system is a buy\\nor sell advice to a human manager. A simulation showed that it outperformed baseline models.\\n\\n3.4        Discussion\\nLLM assistants and robots are a core part of agentic LLM research. Their ability to perform concrete actions in\\nthe real world has also attracted commercial interest. LLMs require tools to be able to act and interact within the\\nworld, and become agentic.\\n  We have surveyed the individual methods for world models and agentic assistants. In order to dig deeper into\\nthe capabilities of LLM assistants, we will now discuss systems to perform scientific research in more detail.\\n3.4.1 In Depth: AI Scientist. We have seen how AI Scientist [Lu et al., 2024a], AI Co Scientist [Schmidgall et al.,\\n2025] and SWE-Agent [Yang et al., 2024b] are used to perform a full scientific and software engineering workflow.\\nGoogle and OpenAI have released Deep Research agents that produce research reports in half an hour’s time.\\nImpressive results are being reported—ideas have been generated, datasets have been downloaded, experiments\\nhave been performed, and scientific articles have been written. Although the field is still young, we will analyze\\nearly results and discuss possible consequences for scientific research.\\n   First, we note that tools such as Alphaxiv are already surprisingly useful for summarizing the content of\\nscientific papers, and offer social community building tools, and LMnotebook can produce readable blogs and\\nreports that summarize the essence of scientific papers remarkably well. Since (1) LLMs are good at text generation,\\n(2) agents can access tools to perform external tasks such as running machine learning experiments, and, thanks\\nto self reflection, (3) agents can learn from their mistakes and try again, it should come as no surprise that certain\\nscientific workflows are a good fit for agentic LLMs. The elements for performing basic scientific experiments\\nand reporting about them are in place.\\n   To what extent are LLM science tools able to perform independent, creative, high quality, research? Tools such\\nas AI Scientist require prompts and templates that are specific for parts of the scientific workflow: idea generation,\\nexperimentation, and paper writing [Lu et al., 2024a]. The prompts and templates are currently hand-written and\\ntailored for the specific type of experiment. We are not aware of any meta-science-tools, where these prompts\\nand templates are generated by an LLM.\\n   To help answer the question how well agentic LLM scientists are able to perform, the MLGym framework\\n[Nathani et al., 2025] has been introduced. MLGym is designed as an open framework to benchmark AI science\\ntools on a range of domains—from data science, game theory, computer vision, natural language processing,\\nand reinforcement learning—all from machine learning. On a test in 2025 with then-current frontier models,\\nthe authors report that prompts and hyperparameter settings are important, and with tuning, results can be\\nimproved. They also report that MLGym did not find that models could generate novel hypotheses, algorithms,\\narchitectures, or make substantial scientific improvements [Nathani et al., 2025]. In particular, the authors note\\nthat modern LLM agents can successfully tackle a diverse array of quantitative experiments, reflecting advanced\\n\\nJournal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\\n\"}]}\n", "stderr": "", "timed_out": false}
{"event": "evidence", "time": 1793244749.1150503, "run_id": "e062ae75f4864af1bc6c029afbd89bab", "seq": 236, "id": "ev-1e23be7c32e2bf283689", "source": "paper.pdf", "version": "b4767423c79db69ae7138f4c0aed7733af6385e0a370da21ad7d6fe029d7fb8c", "locator": "page:27", "text": "                                                                                                              Agentic LLM     •   1:27\n\n\n   Another kind of assistants are research agents, that gather information by combining information from\ndifferent tools and knowledge sources based on context, and synthesize and summarize the feedback based on\nthe information need. For instance, Vogt et al. [2025] developed a REWOO-based process mining assistant that\ncombines a process discovery agent to discover processes and inefficiencies with parallel process research agents,\nan optional human-in-the-loop to interpret results from a domain-specific real-world knowledge perspective,\nand a reporting agent to summarize results. Research agents are popular initial use cases. They are relatively low\nrisk, as actions are limited to various tools to gather data and content, and they build on the strengths of LLMs to\nanalyze intent and synthesize information rather than being the knowledge source itself.\n   Conversational assistants have mostly grown out of regular LLMs, sometimes finetuned, grounded and\ncustomized for a particular area of expertise or domain. Some approaches use a specialized multi-LLMs approach,\nspecializing LLMs for different sub-tasks.\n   Shopping Assistants. LLM-based shopping assistants grow out of regular LLMs that are often finetuned on the\ndomain or task at hand, and may be combined with a recommender system. Retrieval augmentation, tool use,\nand Chain of Thought are used to improve the performance of shopping assistants.\n   Basic LLMs generally lack inherent knowledge of e-commerce concepts. Jin et al. [2024b] created the Multi task\nOnline Shopping Benchmark. Shopping MMLU consists of 57 tasks covering 4 major shopping skills: concept\nunderstanding, knowledge reasoning, user behavior alignment, and multi-linguality. Vedula et al. [2024] provide\nquestion suggestion for shopping assistants based on product metadata. ChatShop presents evaluation focused on\ninformation-seeking [Chen et al., 2024a]. Zhang et al. [2024a] created an E-commerce shopping assistant named\nLLaSa. They created an instruction dataset comprising 65,000 samples and diverse tasks, and trained the model\nthrough instruction tuning. The system scores high on the ShopBench benchmark.\n   Automated negotiation between agents has been studied extensively in AI [Jonker et al., 2017]. LLM based\nnegotiation introduces a risk of unexpected bias. A study by Kirshner et al. [2025] notes a tendency towards reach-\ning agreement, which may influence contract terms. A further experimental analysis finds risks of overspending\nor unreasonable deals for automated negotiation [Zhu et al., 2025]. Section 4.1.2 also discusses negotiation, in a\nsocial setting.\n   Flight Operations Assistants. Assistants for booking flights are related to shopping assistants. [Manasa et al.,\n2024] have developed a flight booking assistant based on LLaMa 2 and RAG. In user testing the system scored\npositive in understanding user preferences and efficient completion of the booking process.\n   In specialized domains, operations support assistant have been developed. For example, to automate flight\nplanning under wind hazards [Tabrizian et al., 2024], and for flight arrival scheduling [Zhou et al., 2024b]. Wassim\net al. [2024] introduce Drone-as-a-Service operations from text user requests. As agentic LLM technology matures,\nmore specialized domain assistants will be developed.\n3.3.2 Medical Assistants. The field of medicine has shown great interest in LLMs [Thirunavukarasu et al., 2023,\nClusmann et al., 2023, Mehandru et al., 2024]. A recent study showed LLMs scoring higher on diagnoses than\ntrained human doctors [Goh et al., 2024]. In medical conversations, for medical note generation, LLMs are also\nexceeding the performance of human scribes [Yuan et al., 2024a]. Another study finds similar results, but also\npoints to shortcomings in specific areas [Panagoulias et al., 2024].\n   Sudarshan et al. [2024] report on an experiment with an agentic workflow for generating patient-friendly\nmedical reports, using the Reflexion approach (Section 2.2.2, [Shinn et al., 2024]), to comply with the 21th Century\nCures Act that grants patients the right to access their health record data.\n   A study by Qiu et al. [2024b] reports a wealth of opportunities for LLMs in medicine, ranging from clinical\nworkflow automation to multi-agent aided diagnosis. Ullah et al. [2024] provide a scoping review on the use of\nChatGPT for diagnostic medicine. Their main conclusion is that medical and ethical knowledge is necessary\n\n                                      Journal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\n", "required": true, "page": 27, "valid": true, "atomic": true}
{"event": "evidence", "time": 1793244749.1151824, "run_id": "e062ae75f4864af1bc6c029afbd89bab", "seq": 237, "id": "ev-eb42e63472799e638c3f", "source": "paper.pdf", "version": "b4767423c79db69ae7138f4c0aed7733af6385e0a370da21ad7d6fe029d7fb8c", "locator": "page:28", "text": "1:28   •   Plaat, Van Duijn, Van Stein, Preuss, Van der Putten & Batenburg\n\n\n\n\n                                        Fig. 17. Medical Education Copilot [Wei et al., 2024a]\n\n\n\nwhen training and finetuning these models. A challenge for the adoption of LLM in medicine are concerns about\nthe quality, accuracy, and the comprehensiveness of LLM-generated answers. Das et al. [2024] describe how to\nmitigate common pitfalls such as hallucinations, incoherence, and lost-in-the-middle problems. They do so by\nimplementing an agentic architecture, changing the LLM’s role from directly generating answers, to that of a\nplanner in a retrieval system. The LLM-agent orchestrates a suite of specialized tools that retrieve information\nfrom various sources.\n   In the domain of medical education, Wei et al. [2024a] use a multi agent framework to create copilots that\nemulate extensive real-world medical training environments (see Figure 17). A benchmark for retrieval-augmented\ngeneration in the medical domain is [Qiao et al., 2024].\n\n3.3.3 Science Assistants. The workflow of scientific experimentation is relatively standardized in certain fields\nof science. For example, in machine learning, ideas are generated, a hypothesis is formulated, an experiment\nis designed, datasets are acquired, experiments are performed, results are interpreted and a report is produced.\nGoogle and OpenAI have both released Deep Research agents. These agents can perform multi-step research\ntasks, synthesizing online information. They are built with a reasoning LLM and use retrieval augmentation for\nfinding information sources. The systems are able to create papers that look impressive, but may contain errors,\nas also indicated by the accompanying disclaimers. This workflow has attracted researchers to experiment with\nagentic LLMs, see Eger et al. [2025] for a survey.\n   AI Scientist [Lu et al., 2024a] is a framework to automate the process of scientific discovery, from idea generation\nto paper writing, including a review process. Users must specify a topic, and provide an experimentation template\nand indicate datasets. The authors report experiments in three areas of machine learning: diffusion modeling,\ntransformer-based language modeling, and learning dynamics, with promising results. To improve idea generation\nand reviewing, the agent accesses open sources. Current limitations include limited experiments, incorrect\nimplementation of ideas, and visual errors when the paper is produced. Rarely are entire results hallucinated.\nResults of the AI scientist are recommended to be taken as hints of promising ideas, worthy of a follow up study\n[Lu et al., 2024a].\n\nJournal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\n", "required": true, "page": 28, "valid": true, "atomic": true}
{"event": "evidence", "time": 1793244749.115225, "run_id": "e062ae75f4864af1bc6c029afbd89bab", "seq": 238, "id": "ev-81117fe71aa5d10b548d", "source": "paper.pdf", "version": "b4767423c79db69ae7138f4c0aed7733af6385e0a370da21ad7d6fe029d7fb8c", "locator": "page:29", "text": "                                                                                                              Agentic LLM     •   1:29\n\n\n\n\n                           Fig. 18. SWE-Agent for software engineering [Yang et al., 2024b]\n\n\n\n   A similar approach was taken in Agent Laboratory, where the agentic system was positioned as a crew of\nresearch assistants, working guided by human researchers across literature review, experimentation, and report\nwriting, and producing experiments, code repositories and a final report [Schmidgall et al., 2025]. The results\nwere evaluated through a survey. Early human involvement was found to improve the quality of research. The\nauthors claim that the generated code outperformed prior results, with a substantial reduction in research effort.\n   SWE-Agent (short for software engineering agent) [Yang et al., 2024b] aims to automate the process of software\ndiscovery, to help agents to autonomously use computers to solve software engineering tasks. SWE-agent\nintroduces tools to create and edit code files, navigate through software repositories, and execute computer\nprograms. Experiments on coding benchmarks such as HumanEvalFix achieve high success rates of over 80%.\nThis success is attributed to the interactive design of the agent (see Figure 18).\n   MLGym [Nathani et al., 2025] follows the popular Gym reinforcement learning framework [Brockman et al.,\n2016]. Gym provides a standardized interface between environment and agent. Introduced in 2016, it accelerated\nthe development of reinforcement learning algorithms, facilitating progress in the field. Taking further inspiration\nfrom SWE-agent (such as file editing capabilities) the MLGym work shows how the process of scientific discovery\ncan be modeled as an interactive process. Applications are discussed in fields ranging from data science, game\ntheory, computer vision, reinforcement learning, to natural language processing. Experiments are reported with\ncommercial LLMs (OpenAI, Meta, Google, and Anthropic).\n   Science assistants are moving from isolated simulations to human/agent research collaborations. Gottweis et al.\n[2025] describe an attempt to discover significant scientific knowledge and validate these findings in real world\nexperiments in their AI Co-scientist work. Given a general research goal or idea, AI Co-scientist uses multiple\nagents for idea generation, reflection, ranking, evolution, proximity and meta-review, ultimately to generate\nresearch hypotheses and plans. Such collaborations were validated in real world laboratory experiments in drug\nrepurposing, novel treatment targets and explaining specific mechanism in gene transfer evolution related to\nmicrobiological resistance [Gottweis et al., 2025, He et al., 2025, Penades et al., 2025].\n   Another example of real world validation is The Virtual Lab. This approach employs a PI agent directing a crew\nof specialized agents in chemistry, computer science and bioinformatics tools, that collaborate with a human\nresearcher to identify new SARS-CoV-2 nanobodies. Promising results in experimental validation were reported\n[Swanson et al., 2024].\n\n3.3.4 Trading Assistants. Another important specialized domain is financial trading. Already many algorithms\nare used in financial organizations to support trading decisions. The interest in agentic LLMs in the financial\nworld is large [Ding et al., 2024].\n   InvestorBench is a benchmark for financial trading systems [Li et al., 2024a]. FinAgent is a tool-augmented\nmultimodal agent for financial trading [Zhang et al., 2024b]. It contains a market intelligence module, which\nis able to extract insights from multi-modal datasets of asset prices, visual representations, news, and expert\n\n                                      Journal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\n", "required": true, "page": 29, "valid": true, "atomic": true}
{"event": "evidence", "time": 1793244749.1152678, "run_id": "e062ae75f4864af1bc6c029afbd89bab", "seq": 239, "id": "ev-97c7764bd93b97111551", "source": "paper.pdf", "version": "b4767423c79db69ae7138f4c0aed7733af6385e0a370da21ad7d6fe029d7fb8c", "locator": "page:30", "text": "1:30   •    Plaat, Van Duijn, Van Stein, Preuss, Van der Putten & Batenburg\n\n\nanalyses. The system can also perform query retrieval, and performs reflection in a low-level module for technical\nanalysis, and in a high-level module to analyze past trading decisions.\n   FinRobot is an agentic LLM for financial analysis, to assist human traders. [Yang et al., 2024a]. FinRobot can\nprovide document analysis and generation, and market forecasts for individual stocks. FinMem is an agentic\nLLM framework devised for financial decision-making [Yu et al., 2024b]. It features a layered memory system\nand adjustable character design for the trading agent. FinMem is inspired by the generative agents framework by\nPark et al. [2023] (see Section 4.3).\n   So far, most financial market machine learning has focused on single agent systems. An approach called\nTradingAgents uses a multi agent system to replicate real-world trading firms’ collaborative dynamics [Xiao\net al., 2024]. TradingAgents simulates LLM-powered agents in specialized roles such as fundamental analysts,\nsentiment analysts, technical analysts, and traders with varied risk profiles. The outcome of the system is a buy\nor sell advice to a human manager. A simulation showed that it outperformed baseline models.\n\n3.4        Discussion\nLLM assistants and robots are a core part of agentic LLM research. Their ability to perform concrete actions in\nthe real world has also attracted commercial interest. LLMs require tools to be able to act and interact within the\nworld, and become agentic.\n  We have surveyed the individual methods for world models and agentic assistants. In order to dig deeper into\nthe capabilities of LLM assistants, we will now discuss systems to perform scientific research in more detail.\n3.4.1 In Depth: AI Scientist. We have seen how AI Scientist [Lu et al., 2024a], AI Co Scientist [Schmidgall et al.,\n2025] and SWE-Agent [Yang et al., 2024b] are used to perform a full scientific and software engineering workflow.\nGoogle and OpenAI have released Deep Research agents that produce research reports in half an hour’s time.\nImpressive results are being reported—ideas have been generated, datasets have been downloaded, experiments\nhave been performed, and scientific articles have been written. Although the field is still young, we will analyze\nearly results and discuss possible consequences for scientific research.\n   First, we note that tools such as Alphaxiv are already surprisingly useful for summarizing the content of\nscientific papers, and offer social community building tools, and LMnotebook can produce readable blogs and\nreports that summarize the essence of scientific papers remarkably well. Since (1) LLMs are good at text generation,\n(2) agents can access tools to perform external tasks such as running machine learning experiments, and, thanks\nto self reflection, (3) agents can learn from their mistakes and try again, it should come as no surprise that certain\nscientific workflows are a good fit for agentic LLMs. The elements for performing basic scientific experiments\nand reporting about them are in place.\n   To what extent are LLM science tools able to perform independent, creative, high quality, research? Tools such\nas AI Scientist require prompts and templates that are specific for parts of the scientific workflow: idea generation,\nexperimentation, and paper writing [Lu et al., 2024a]. The prompts and templates are currently hand-written and\ntailored for the specific type of experiment. We are not aware of any meta-science-tools, where these prompts\nand templates are generated by an LLM.\n   To help answer the question how well agentic LLM scientists are able to perform, the MLGym framework\n[Nathani et al., 2025] has been introduced. MLGym is designed as an open framework to benchmark AI science\ntools on a range of domains—from data science, game theory, computer vision, natural language processing,\nand reinforcement learning—all from machine learning. On a test in 2025 with then-current frontier models,\nthe authors report that prompts and hyperparameter settings are important, and with tuning, results can be\nimproved. They also report that MLGym did not find that models could generate novel hypotheses, algorithms,\narchitectures, or make substantial scientific improvements [Nathani et al., 2025]. In particular, the authors note\nthat modern LLM agents can successfully tackle a diverse array of quantitative experiments, reflecting advanced\n\nJournal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\n", "required": true, "page": 30, "valid": true, "atomic": true}
