{"event": "tool_call", "time": 1793244801.1832545, "run_id": "e062ae75f4864af1bc6c029afbd89bab", "seq": 414, "tool": "read_pdf", "arguments": {"start_page": 47, "end_page": 49}}
{"event": "command_result", "time": 1793244801.2881691, "run_id": "e062ae75f4864af1bc6c029afbd89bab", "seq": 415, "argv": ["/mnt/d/Muiti-Agent Programing/experiments/code-agent-smolagents/24371220/.venv/bin/python", "read_pdf.py", "--start-page", "47", "--end-page", "49"], "cwd": "/home/ventsdenye/.local/state/code-agent/evaluations/run-3rn9_ftz/results/paper/repo", "rc": 0, "stdout": "{\"pdf\": \"/home/ventsdenye/.local/state/code-agent/evaluations/run-3rn9_ftz/results/paper/repo/paper.pdf\", \"pages\": [{\"page\": 47, \"text\": \"                                                                                                               Agentic LLM     •   1:47\\n\\n\\nthat manage constantly-growing memories as new interactions, conflicts, and events arise and fade over time while\\nhandling cascading social dynamics that unfold between multiple agents, and that success requires an approach that\\ncan retrieve relevant events and interactions over a long period, reflect on those memories to generalize and draw\\nhigher-level inferences, and apply that reasoning to create plans and reactions that make sense in the moment and in\\nthe longer-term of the agent’s behavior. The LLM that is used is GPT-3.5, the same as in CAMEL.\\n   Whether the Generative Agents system generates believable behavior is evaluated with the help of 100 human\\nevaluators. These were enlisted to rank believability of the communication patterns on four categories, by\\ninterviewing the agents to probe their ability to (1) remember past experiences, (2) plan future actions based on\\ntheir experiences, (3) react appropriately to unexpected events, and (4) reflect on their performance to improve\\ntheir future actions. The evaluation studied the results of four different agent architectures (full architecture, and\\nno observation, no refection, no planning), and found that the full architecture performed best. They also found\\nthat generative agents remember with embellishments, and that reflection is required for synthesis of memories.\\nThe evaluation did find evidence of emerging communication, relationship building, and coordination. They also\\nfound evidence of erratic behavior, hallucination, and misclassification, such as agents that were trying to enter\\nstores after closing time, not understanding the concept of closing time.\\n   Simulating 1000 People. We will now turn to the second paper [Park et al., 2024]. Multi-agent simulations can be\\nused to study different aspects of emergent individual and social human behavior. An important methodological\\nchallenge in prompt-based simulation studies is to determine how much of the behavior is scripted, and how\\nmuch emerges. Park et al. [2024] focus in the second study on how realistic the behavior of synthetic agents can\\nbe. The study is based on their earlier work, with LLM agents that have memory and reflection. A group of 1052\\nhuman individuals were recruited who were asked to provide a two hour long interview. The interviews were\\nstandardized, administered by an AI interviewer. Next, LLM agents were trained on the audio interview, yielding\\n1052 different LLM agent profiles. The LLM is prompted to replicate individuals’ attitudes and behaviors and\\ngenerate synthetic agents.\\n   To validate the accuracy of the personality and behavior of these generative synthetic agents, the agents were\\ntested by interviewing them. As a control group, the human subjects were also interviewed, again, two weeks\\nafter their initial interview, to control for natural variation between two interview sessions. The generative\\nagents replicate participants’ responses on the General Social Survey 85% as accurately as participants replicate\\ntheir own answers two weeks later, and perform comparably in predicting personality traits and outcomes in\\nexperimental replications. Subsequently, the synthetic agents have been made available for further experiments.\\n4.4.3 Emergent Collective Behavior. Emergent behavior, and especially emergent cooperation, is an important\\nuse case of agentic LLMs. It helps us understand our own behavior in our society, and allows the study of agent\\nbehavior in artificial conditions, in what-if scenarios. When do we benefit from more competition, when from\\nmore cooperation, and in what form? What happens when (fake) information disseminates? Or how do societies\\nrespond to extreme circumstances, such as a natural disaster?\\n   As research on collective agent societies and emergent phenomena develops further, LLMs will exhibit more\\nrealistic behavior, new multi-agent infrastructures will be developed that allow more diverse types of interactions,\\nand simulation studies will provide insight into social science questions. In particular, topics of interest are the\\ninfluence of LLMs on democratic processes and cyber security, role playing, society of minds, theory of mind,\\ncurriculum learning, continuous learning, adversarial agents, and collaboration in the face of hierarchy.\\n   Furthermore, as our understanding of the conditions conducive to emergence of cooperation grows, a focus on\\nadaptive (social) intelligence may influence our views on the nature of intelligence and artificial (super)intelligence.\\n4.4.4 New Training Data. A final use case of this third part of the taxonomy is that new training data is generated\\nby the interacting agents. Traditionally, LLMs are trained on a large static corpus of language data, that is taken\\n\\n                                       Journal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\\n\"}, {\"page\": 48, \"text\": \"1:48   •    Plaat, Van Duijn, Van Stein, Preuss, Van der Putten & Batenburg\\n\\n\\nfrom the internet, and ultimately based on human actions, using self-supervised learning methods. As illustrated\\nby the cycle in Figure 1, interacting agentic LLMs enable self-learning, in the style of reinforcement learning.\\nReinforcement learning is used increasingly in LLM training, for example to train reasoning models by OpenAI\\n[Huang et al., 2024c, Wu et al., 2024], and DeepSeek [Guo et al., 2025]. New reinforcement learning methods such\\nas GRPO [Shao et al., 2024] and RLVR [Lambert et al., 2024] already allow inference-time chains of thoughts to\\nbe used for finetuning.\\n   In reinforcement learning, agents choose their own actions in the world, and are not limited by a pre-existing\\ndataset. In principle, they can learn the full complexity of the world, including the effects of their own actions.\\nA challenge in reinforcement learning is the instability caused by feedback loops. Past reinforcement learning\\nsuccesses have achieved stable training through diverse exploration and low learning rates, requiring large\\ncomputational efforts [Silver et al., 2016, Vinyals et al., 2019, Brown and Sandholm, 2019]. Open-ended and\\nopen-world multi-agent simulation may provide an alternative way to create the necessary diversity for stable\\nconvergence.\\n\\n5      General Discussion and Research Agenda\\nThe interest in agentic LLMs is large, and many research efforts have appeared over a short period. We have\\nreviewed the field, with an emphasis on the most recent works.\\n   First, there is an interest from society in agentic LLMs. Agentic LLMs can assist us in our daily lives in many\\nways—from writing essays, booking flights, having pleasant and interesting conversations, folding our laundry,\\nto making better medical diagnoses, performing better stock analyses, to support healthy lifestyle changes, to\\nmake sure we take our medicine, to assist us when we are less mobile. Tool use by assistants is enabled by\\ntechnology from the first category of the taxonomy: reasoning LLMs, self-reflection and retrieval augmentation.\\nBoth reasoning and tool use support new forms of interaction, with human and artificial agents, further enhancing\\nsocietal applicability of LLMs.\\n   Second, there is an interest from science in agentic LLMs, inside the AI research community and beyond. Since\\nLLM agents can now interact in natural language, agent behavior can be better understood, and multi-agent\\nsimulations can be made more realistic than before. Important questions in social and political science can be\\nresearched, such as in game theory (social dilemmas), social interaction (negotiation, theory of mind), and societal\\ndynamics (cooperation, norms, extreme situations). Some of these goals are within reach, some have been realized\\nalready, and some are becoming a possibility. Also in research applications, agent interactions are enabled by the\\nprevious two categories: social behavior benefits from reasoning and self reflection, social actions are increasingly\\ngrounded, and information can be retrieved to further enhance understanding of social contexts.\\n   Finally, agentic LLMs generate data that can augment inference-time behavior and on which models can\\nbe further pretrained and finetuned, improving LLMs beyond the plateau researchers have observed recently.\\nFigure 1 illustrates this cycle of continuous improvement.\\n\\n5.1        Research Agenda for Agentic LLM\\nOur survey has yielded interesting directions for a research agenda for agentic LLMs, which we will now discuss\\nin more detail. Please refer to Table 4 for a summary of the agenda.\\n  Training Data. The benefit from language corpuses that are used for pretraining of LLMs is said to be plateauing.\\nTo improve the performance of LLMs on language (and reasoning) tasks further, it is important to continue to\\nacquire training data that is sufficiently novel and challenging from a token-prediction point of view. Such data\\ncan be generated by making LLMs interact with the world at inference time.\\n  Currently, in most approaches that were discussed in Section 2, inference-time compute is only used to improve\\nperformance on reasoning benchmarks. In most early Chain of Thought approaches the generated data is not\\n\\nJournal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\\n\"}, {\"page\": 49, \"text\": \"                                                                                                                 Agentic LLM     •   1:49\\n\\n\\n                                                     Agent: 𝐿𝐿𝑀 → 𝜋\\n\\n                                     𝑠         𝑟                                         𝑎\\n\\n                                                        Environment\\n\\n                             Fig. 32. LLM as the policy of a Reinforcement Learning agent\\n\\n\\n\\nused after the answer has been calculated. In other approaches—such as Say Can, Inner Monologue, and Vision-\\nLanguage-Action models—data that is generated at inference time is used for augmentation of the finetuning\\ndataset, creating an inference time-finetuning feedback loop, so that the model’s parameters are trained from its\\nown earlier reasoning.\\n   Such feedback loops are common in reinforcement learning, where agents act and receive feedback from their\\nenvironment. In games, a self-learning loop can be created [Plaat, 2022]. In AlphaGo Zero this approach yielded\\ngood results, although at the cost of careful tuning of hyperparameters and algorithms, to ensure sustained\\nconvergence of the learning process [Silver et al., 2017]. Similar results were achieved in other challenging games,\\nsuch as StarCraft [Vinyals et al., 2019], Stratego [Perolat et al., 2022], DOTA 2 [Berner et al., 2019], Diplomacy\\n[Bakhtin et al., 2022], and Poker [Brown and Sandholm, 2019].\\n   More formally, in the traditional self-supervised view a model 𝑀 is trained to predict label 𝑦 from input variable\\n𝑥 in dataset 𝐷; in reinforcement learning an agent’s policy 𝜋 is trained with reward 𝑟 to perform action 𝑎 to\\nchange state 𝑠 of its environment 𝐸. In agentic LLMs, both views are joined. Agentic LLMs use a language model\\n𝑀 as the policy 𝜋 to determine the agent’s next action (see Figure 32). Actions can be used to retrieve information,\\nto split a larger problem into smaller parts, to run a tool, to use memory to reflect on its own actions, to suggest\\nstock trades, to book travel tickets, or to interact with other agents working towards a common goal.\\n   The approach that worked well in games of strategy is now also successfully used in robotics, in the creation of\\nVision-Language-Action models [Black et al., 2024, Brohan et al., 2023]. VLAs that are trained on self-generated\\naction sequences show zero-shot generalization results in domestic tasks (kitchen tasks, folding laundry) that\\nhad not been achieved by other machine learning methods. Recently, reasoning models—such as DeepSeek [Guo\\net al., 2025] and Kimi [Du et al., 2025]—are also being trained with reinforcement learning. Popular methods for\\nfinetuning for mathematics and coding tasks are GRPO [Shao et al., 2024] and RLVR [Lambert et al., 2024]. Other\\nuses of agents for finetuning are reported by Subramaniam et al. [2025].\\n   Reuse of inference time results for finetuning and pretraining closes the learning loop (see Figure 1), and is the\\nfirst item for the agenda for further research. It is interesting to see how the reinforcement learning methods that\\nworked well for games of strategy and certain finetuning tasks, are being translated to work in LLMs that act in\\nthe real world.\\n   VLAs integrate multiple modalities: language, visual information, and actions. Further modalities are speech,\\nother audio signals, and videos. Electrical signals, such as brain or muscle activation, can also provide valuable\\ninputs for the models to learn from.\\n\\n  Hallucination and Stable Behavior. A challenge for the virtuous autocurriculum cycle is that LLMs hallucinate,\\nand in multi-step reasoning errors can easily accumulate. LLM answers may look good, but be factually wrong.\\nReasoning chains may be unfaithful, giving good answers for the wrong reason, and wrong answers when least\\nexpected. Especially when such dubious results are used to further train the LLM, this training may diverge and\\nmodel collapse may occur. In social simulations, emerging behavior patterns, such as cooperation, fairness, trust\\nor norms, may collapse. Therefore, in multi-step reasoning, self verification and self consistency methods were\\n\\n                                         Journal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\\n\"}]}\n", "stderr": "", "timed_out": false}
{"event": "evidence", "time": 1793244801.2976027, "run_id": "e062ae75f4864af1bc6c029afbd89bab", "seq": 416, "id": "ev-f03e4b7f1b7fc6e66886", "source": "paper.pdf", "version": "b4767423c79db69ae7138f4c0aed7733af6385e0a370da21ad7d6fe029d7fb8c", "locator": "page:47", "text": "                                                                                                               Agentic LLM     •   1:47\n\n\nthat manage constantly-growing memories as new interactions, conflicts, and events arise and fade over time while\nhandling cascading social dynamics that unfold between multiple agents, and that success requires an approach that\ncan retrieve relevant events and interactions over a long period, reflect on those memories to generalize and draw\nhigher-level inferences, and apply that reasoning to create plans and reactions that make sense in the moment and in\nthe longer-term of the agent’s behavior. The LLM that is used is GPT-3.5, the same as in CAMEL.\n   Whether the Generative Agents system generates believable behavior is evaluated with the help of 100 human\nevaluators. These were enlisted to rank believability of the communication patterns on four categories, by\ninterviewing the agents to probe their ability to (1) remember past experiences, (2) plan future actions based on\ntheir experiences, (3) react appropriately to unexpected events, and (4) reflect on their performance to improve\ntheir future actions. The evaluation studied the results of four different agent architectures (full architecture, and\nno observation, no refection, no planning), and found that the full architecture performed best. They also found\nthat generative agents remember with embellishments, and that reflection is required for synthesis of memories.\nThe evaluation did find evidence of emerging communication, relationship building, and coordination. They also\nfound evidence of erratic behavior, hallucination, and misclassification, such as agents that were trying to enter\nstores after closing time, not understanding the concept of closing time.\n   Simulating 1000 People. We will now turn to the second paper [Park et al., 2024]. Multi-agent simulations can be\nused to study different aspects of emergent individual and social human behavior. An important methodological\nchallenge in prompt-based simulation studies is to determine how much of the behavior is scripted, and how\nmuch emerges. Park et al. [2024] focus in the second study on how realistic the behavior of synthetic agents can\nbe. The study is based on their earlier work, with LLM agents that have memory and reflection. A group of 1052\nhuman individuals were recruited who were asked to provide a two hour long interview. The interviews were\nstandardized, administered by an AI interviewer. Next, LLM agents were trained on the audio interview, yielding\n1052 different LLM agent profiles. The LLM is prompted to replicate individuals’ attitudes and behaviors and\ngenerate synthetic agents.\n   To validate the accuracy of the personality and behavior of these generative synthetic agents, the agents were\ntested by interviewing them. As a control group, the human subjects were also interviewed, again, two weeks\nafter their initial interview, to control for natural variation between two interview sessions. The generative\nagents replicate participants’ responses on the General Social Survey 85% as accurately as participants replicate\ntheir own answers two weeks later, and perform comparably in predicting personality traits and outcomes in\nexperimental replications. Subsequently, the synthetic agents have been made available for further experiments.\n4.4.3 Emergent Collective Behavior. Emergent behavior, and especially emergent cooperation, is an important\nuse case of agentic LLMs. It helps us understand our own behavior in our society, and allows the study of agent\nbehavior in artificial conditions, in what-if scenarios. When do we benefit from more competition, when from\nmore cooperation, and in what form? What happens when (fake) information disseminates? Or how do societies\nrespond to extreme circumstances, such as a natural disaster?\n   As research on collective agent societies and emergent phenomena develops further, LLMs will exhibit more\nrealistic behavior, new multi-agent infrastructures will be developed that allow more diverse types of interactions,\nand simulation studies will provide insight into social science questions. In particular, topics of interest are the\ninfluence of LLMs on democratic processes and cyber security, role playing, society of minds, theory of mind,\ncurriculum learning, continuous learning, adversarial agents, and collaboration in the face of hierarchy.\n   Furthermore, as our understanding of the conditions conducive to emergence of cooperation grows, a focus on\nadaptive (social) intelligence may influence our views on the nature of intelligence and artificial (super)intelligence.\n4.4.4 New Training Data. A final use case of this third part of the taxonomy is that new training data is generated\nby the interacting agents. Traditionally, LLMs are trained on a large static corpus of language data, that is taken\n\n                                       Journal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\n", "required": true, "page": 47, "valid": true, "atomic": true}
{"event": "evidence", "time": 1793244801.2977183, "run_id": "e062ae75f4864af1bc6c029afbd89bab", "seq": 417, "id": "ev-aa9b9dbdfec8c640b495", "source": "paper.pdf", "version": "b4767423c79db69ae7138f4c0aed7733af6385e0a370da21ad7d6fe029d7fb8c", "locator": "page:48", "text": "1:48   •    Plaat, Van Duijn, Van Stein, Preuss, Van der Putten & Batenburg\n\n\nfrom the internet, and ultimately based on human actions, using self-supervised learning methods. As illustrated\nby the cycle in Figure 1, interacting agentic LLMs enable self-learning, in the style of reinforcement learning.\nReinforcement learning is used increasingly in LLM training, for example to train reasoning models by OpenAI\n[Huang et al., 2024c, Wu et al., 2024], and DeepSeek [Guo et al., 2025]. New reinforcement learning methods such\nas GRPO [Shao et al., 2024] and RLVR [Lambert et al., 2024] already allow inference-time chains of thoughts to\nbe used for finetuning.\n   In reinforcement learning, agents choose their own actions in the world, and are not limited by a pre-existing\ndataset. In principle, they can learn the full complexity of the world, including the effects of their own actions.\nA challenge in reinforcement learning is the instability caused by feedback loops. Past reinforcement learning\nsuccesses have achieved stable training through diverse exploration and low learning rates, requiring large\ncomputational efforts [Silver et al., 2016, Vinyals et al., 2019, Brown and Sandholm, 2019]. Open-ended and\nopen-world multi-agent simulation may provide an alternative way to create the necessary diversity for stable\nconvergence.\n\n5      General Discussion and Research Agenda\nThe interest in agentic LLMs is large, and many research efforts have appeared over a short period. We have\nreviewed the field, with an emphasis on the most recent works.\n   First, there is an interest from society in agentic LLMs. Agentic LLMs can assist us in our daily lives in many\nways—from writing essays, booking flights, having pleasant and interesting conversations, folding our laundry,\nto making better medical diagnoses, performing better stock analyses, to support healthy lifestyle changes, to\nmake sure we take our medicine, to assist us when we are less mobile. Tool use by assistants is enabled by\ntechnology from the first category of the taxonomy: reasoning LLMs, self-reflection and retrieval augmentation.\nBoth reasoning and tool use support new forms of interaction, with human and artificial agents, further enhancing\nsocietal applicability of LLMs.\n   Second, there is an interest from science in agentic LLMs, inside the AI research community and beyond. Since\nLLM agents can now interact in natural language, agent behavior can be better understood, and multi-agent\nsimulations can be made more realistic than before. Important questions in social and political science can be\nresearched, such as in game theory (social dilemmas), social interaction (negotiation, theory of mind), and societal\ndynamics (cooperation, norms, extreme situations). Some of these goals are within reach, some have been realized\nalready, and some are becoming a possibility. Also in research applications, agent interactions are enabled by the\nprevious two categories: social behavior benefits from reasoning and self reflection, social actions are increasingly\ngrounded, and information can be retrieved to further enhance understanding of social contexts.\n   Finally, agentic LLMs generate data that can augment inference-time behavior and on which models can\nbe further pretrained and finetuned, improving LLMs beyond the plateau researchers have observed recently.\nFigure 1 illustrates this cycle of continuous improvement.\n\n5.1        Research Agenda for Agentic LLM\nOur survey has yielded interesting directions for a research agenda for agentic LLMs, which we will now discuss\nin more detail. Please refer to Table 4 for a summary of the agenda.\n  Training Data. The benefit from language corpuses that are used for pretraining of LLMs is said to be plateauing.\nTo improve the performance of LLMs on language (and reasoning) tasks further, it is important to continue to\nacquire training data that is sufficiently novel and challenging from a token-prediction point of view. Such data\ncan be generated by making LLMs interact with the world at inference time.\n  Currently, in most approaches that were discussed in Section 2, inference-time compute is only used to improve\nperformance on reasoning benchmarks. In most early Chain of Thought approaches the generated data is not\n\nJournal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\n", "required": true, "page": 48, "valid": true, "atomic": true}
{"event": "evidence", "time": 1793244801.2977715, "run_id": "e062ae75f4864af1bc6c029afbd89bab", "seq": 418, "id": "ev-6e64db9ec6bfa8f9417f", "source": "paper.pdf", "version": "b4767423c79db69ae7138f4c0aed7733af6385e0a370da21ad7d6fe029d7fb8c", "locator": "page:49", "text": "                                                                                                                 Agentic LLM     •   1:49\n\n\n                                                     Agent: 𝐿𝐿𝑀 → 𝜋\n\n                                     𝑠         𝑟                                         𝑎\n\n                                                        Environment\n\n                             Fig. 32. LLM as the policy of a Reinforcement Learning agent\n\n\n\nused after the answer has been calculated. In other approaches—such as Say Can, Inner Monologue, and Vision-\nLanguage-Action models—data that is generated at inference time is used for augmentation of the finetuning\ndataset, creating an inference time-finetuning feedback loop, so that the model’s parameters are trained from its\nown earlier reasoning.\n   Such feedback loops are common in reinforcement learning, where agents act and receive feedback from their\nenvironment. In games, a self-learning loop can be created [Plaat, 2022]. In AlphaGo Zero this approach yielded\ngood results, although at the cost of careful tuning of hyperparameters and algorithms, to ensure sustained\nconvergence of the learning process [Silver et al., 2017]. Similar results were achieved in other challenging games,\nsuch as StarCraft [Vinyals et al., 2019], Stratego [Perolat et al., 2022], DOTA 2 [Berner et al., 2019], Diplomacy\n[Bakhtin et al., 2022], and Poker [Brown and Sandholm, 2019].\n   More formally, in the traditional self-supervised view a model 𝑀 is trained to predict label 𝑦 from input variable\n𝑥 in dataset 𝐷; in reinforcement learning an agent’s policy 𝜋 is trained with reward 𝑟 to perform action 𝑎 to\nchange state 𝑠 of its environment 𝐸. In agentic LLMs, both views are joined. Agentic LLMs use a language model\n𝑀 as the policy 𝜋 to determine the agent’s next action (see Figure 32). Actions can be used to retrieve information,\nto split a larger problem into smaller parts, to run a tool, to use memory to reflect on its own actions, to suggest\nstock trades, to book travel tickets, or to interact with other agents working towards a common goal.\n   The approach that worked well in games of strategy is now also successfully used in robotics, in the creation of\nVision-Language-Action models [Black et al., 2024, Brohan et al., 2023]. VLAs that are trained on self-generated\naction sequences show zero-shot generalization results in domestic tasks (kitchen tasks, folding laundry) that\nhad not been achieved by other machine learning methods. Recently, reasoning models—such as DeepSeek [Guo\net al., 2025] and Kimi [Du et al., 2025]—are also being trained with reinforcement learning. Popular methods for\nfinetuning for mathematics and coding tasks are GRPO [Shao et al., 2024] and RLVR [Lambert et al., 2024]. Other\nuses of agents for finetuning are reported by Subramaniam et al. [2025].\n   Reuse of inference time results for finetuning and pretraining closes the learning loop (see Figure 1), and is the\nfirst item for the agenda for further research. It is interesting to see how the reinforcement learning methods that\nworked well for games of strategy and certain finetuning tasks, are being translated to work in LLMs that act in\nthe real world.\n   VLAs integrate multiple modalities: language, visual information, and actions. Further modalities are speech,\nother audio signals, and videos. Electrical signals, such as brain or muscle activation, can also provide valuable\ninputs for the models to learn from.\n\n  Hallucination and Stable Behavior. A challenge for the virtuous autocurriculum cycle is that LLMs hallucinate,\nand in multi-step reasoning errors can easily accumulate. LLM answers may look good, but be factually wrong.\nReasoning chains may be unfaithful, giving good answers for the wrong reason, and wrong answers when least\nexpected. Especially when such dubious results are used to further train the LLM, this training may diverge and\nmodel collapse may occur. In social simulations, emerging behavior patterns, such as cooperation, fairness, trust\nor norms, may collapse. Therefore, in multi-step reasoning, self verification and self consistency methods were\n\n                                         Journal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\n", "required": true, "page": 49, "valid": true, "atomic": true}
